# Noto — line endings.
#
# LF is the canonical form in the repository. Git normalises text on commit and
# checks it out as LF, so the stored bytes are the same whatever a contributor's
# core.autocrlf happens to be. .editorconfig tells editors the same thing; this
# file is what actually enforces it, because .editorconfig only binds tools that
# read it.
#
# Why this exists: the repository was CRLF because .editorconfig declared
# `end_of_line = crlf` and `dotnet format` enforced it. Every `git diff --check`
# then reported a trailing-whitespace "artefact" on every line of every file,
# which trains reviewers to ignore that check — the opposite of what it is for.

# Default: treat as text, store LF, check out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never normalise. None of these are tracked today; they are listed so
# that adding one later cannot corrupt it silently.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.7z     binary
*.gz     binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.pdb    binary
*.snk    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary

# SQLite databases, should one ever be committed (tests create their own in a
# temp directory, and the user's lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%).
*.db     binary
*.db-shm binary
*.db-wal binary
*.sqlite binary
